# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/between.grim
# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after "="; keep {tokens}.
# "# en:" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;
# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.


@event between_two_seats
# en: Two Saved Seats
title [a606ac] = 两个留好的座位
# en: {~{a} has put a bag on the bench beside {them}, and at the far end {b} has put a book on the bench too. Both are for you.|{a} waves you over to a saved place. So, from the other end of the table, does {b}. They have not looked at each other once.|There are two places kept for you in the refectory, one at each end of the table, and the people keeping them would rather eat soup through a straw than sit together.}
text [ef3dfc] = {~{a}在{their}身边的长凳上放了个书包，桌子另一头，{b}也在长凳上放了本书。两个位子都是给你留的。|{a}朝你招手，示意给你留了位子。桌子另一头的{b}也在朝你招手。他们俩一眼都没看过对方。|膳堂里有两个位子给你留着，桌子两头各一个；留位子的两个人宁可用吸管喝汤，也不肯坐到一块儿。}
# en: A long refectory table at mealtime, two students at opposite ends, each with a hand on an empty stretch of bench.
scene [6ecc4a] = 饭点时膳堂里一张长桌，两个学生各坐一头，各自用手按着身边一段空长凳。
# en: Sit with {a}
opt.left.label [1864e2] = 坐到{a}那边
# en: {~{a} moves the bag. Down the table, {b} moves the book back onto the bench, slowly, so you see.|{a} has saved you the crust. {b} eats facing the window.|You sit with {a}. {b} leaves before pudding, loudly.}
opt.left.say.1 [35ac8c] = {~{a}挪开了书包。桌子那头，{b}慢慢地把书放回长凳上，故意让你看见。|{a}把面包皮给你留着。{b}一直对着窗户吃饭。|你坐到了{a}身边。{b}没等到布丁就走了，动静还不小。}
# en: Pull them to one table
opt.right.label [6fedd1] = 把他们拉到一张桌上
# en: {~You carry your tray to the middle and wave them both in. They come, sulking, and argue about the gravy, which is at least talking.|You sit dead centre and start a story that needs both of them to finish it. By the end they're correcting each other instead of glaring.|You make them pass you things. Salt, bread, the jug. By the end they're passing each other things, which is how it starts.}
opt.right.say.1 [4593c4] = {~你端着餐盘坐到中间，招手让两人都过来。他们来了，板着脸，为肉汁吵了一架——好歹算是在说话。|你坐在正中间，讲了个非得两个人一起才讲得完的故事。讲到最后，他们已经从瞪眼变成互相纠正了。|你让他们给你递东西。盐、面包、水壶。到最后，他们开始互相递东西了——事情往往就是这么开始的。}
# en: {~You sit in the middle and they both stay where they are. You eat alone between two empty places, like a referee nobody asked for.|You try. {a} takes {their} tray to the window, {b} takes {b.theirs} to the door, and you're left with three puddings.|Neither of them moves. The whole table watches you eat. It's the longest meal of the term.}
opt.right.say.2 [d64c84] = {~你坐到中间，可他们俩谁也没动。你一个人夹在两个空位之间吃饭，像个没人请来的裁判。|你试了。{a}把{their}餐盘端去了窗边，{b}把{b.theirs}端去了门口，留下你和三份布丁。|谁都没动。整张桌子都看着你吃饭。这是本学期最漫长的一顿饭。}

@event between_messenger
# en: Pass It On
title [2bd77a] = 帮忙传个话
# en: {~{a} and {b} aren't speaking, and you've become the post. "Tell {b} the common-room lamp is NOT {b.theirs}," says {a}.|{a} hands you a folded note for {b}. It isn't sealed. The first word is underlined three times.|"Would you let {b} know," says {a}, very politely, "that some of us have to sleep."}
text [79e5de] = {~{a}和{b}互不说话，你成了他们的邮差。“告诉{b}，公共休息室那盏灯可不是{b.theirs}，”{a}说。|{a}递给你一张折好的纸条，让你交给{b}。没封口。第一个词下面划了三道线。|“能不能请你转告{b}，”{a}非常客气地说，“有些人是要睡觉的。”}
# en: A student in a corridor holding a folded note out at arm's length, another student turned pointedly away at the far end.
scene [ae1c70] = 走廊里，一个学生伸长胳膊递出一张折好的纸条，走廊另一头，另一个学生刻意背过身去。
# en: Pass it on word for word
opt.left.label [9c53c3] = 一字不差地转达
# en: {~You deliver it exactly. {b} sends one back, worse. By supper you've carried six, and you're tired in a way that has nothing to do with stairs.|You hand it over unread. {b} reads it, writes "NO" under it, and hands it back to you to carry.|You say it word for word. {b} says something word for word back. You're not paid enough for this, and you're not paid.}
opt.left.say.1 [8b0885] = {~你一字不差地带了过去。{b}回了一张，措辞更难听。到晚饭时你已经跑了六趟，累得和爬楼梯毫无关系。|你没看就交了过去。{b}读完，在下面写了个“不”，又交给你带回去。|你一字一句地转达了。{b}也一字一句地回了话。这份差事给的钱不够——何况根本没钱。}
# en: Soften it on the way
opt.right.label [90adec] = 路上替他们改软一点
# en: {~By the time it reaches {b} it's a polite question about the lamp. {b} answers politely. {a} is baffled, and a little disarmed.|You lose the underlining somewhere on the stairs. {b} reads a reasonable note and writes a reasonable answer. Nobody knows why it worked.|"Would you mind" becomes "would you mind, please". It's enough. They nod at each other at breakfast.}
opt.right.say.1 [122ed5] = {~等传到{b}手里，已经变成一句关于那盏灯的客气询问。{b}也客气地答了。{a}一头雾水，气也消了一点。|那几道下划线不知在哪段楼梯上弄丢了。{b}读到一张讲理的纸条，回了一张讲理的答复。谁也不知道怎么就成了。|“你介意吗”变成了“请问你介意吗”。这就够了。第二天早饭时，两人互相点了点头。}
# en: {~They compare notes, literally. Neither recognises what you delivered. For once they agree on something: you.|{b} asks {a} where the underlining went. Both of them turn to look at you.|It comes out at supper. They're united, briefly, in being annoyed with the post.}
opt.right.say.2 [1283cb] = {~他们真的拿纸条对了一遍。谁都认不出你送去的是什么。难得一回，两人意见一致：都冲着你来。|{b}问{a}那几道下划线哪儿去了。两人一起转过头来看你。|晚饭时事情败露了。他们短暂地团结了起来——一起生邮差的气。}

@event between_whose_idea
# en: Whose Idea Was It
title [43cb9c] = 到底是谁的主意
# en: The lecturer praises the trick that made last week's practical work, and asks whose it was. {a} says "mine", and so does {b}, at the same moment. You were there when {a} thought of it, on the back of an envelope. Both of them look at you.
text [6fc982] = 讲师表扬了让上周实践课成功的那个窍门，问是谁想出来的。{a}说“我”，{b}也在同一刻说“我”。{a}在信封背面想出这个主意时，你就在旁边。两人都看向了你。
# en: Two students in front of a lecturer's desk, both pointing at the same page of notes, a third student standing just behind them.
scene [957c85] = 讲台前站着两个学生，都指着同一页笔记，第三个学生就站在他们身后。
# en: "It was {a}'s."
opt.left.label [d6a4d8] = “是{a}想的。”
# en: {b} goes red to the ears. {a} gets the praise and, later, buys you a cake. {b} doesn't speak to either of you until the end of the week, and is extremely polite when {b.their} silence ends.
opt.left.say.1 [722392] = {b}红到了耳根。{a}得了表扬，后来还请你吃了块蛋糕。{b}直到周末都不跟你们俩说话，沉默结束后又客气得出奇。
# en: "We worked it out together."
opt.right.label [c06886] = “是我们一起想出来的。”
# en: The lecturer gives the three of you the credit and moves on. {b} looks relieved. {a} looks at you for a long moment, and doesn't mention the envelope again.
opt.right.say.1 [c549cc] = 讲师把功劳记在你们三个人头上，接着讲课。{b}松了口气。{a}看了你很久，后来再没提过那个信封。

@event between_boot_hex
# en: Dancing Boots
title [425b3a] = 跳舞的靴子
# en: {~{b}'s boots have started to dance, on their own, in the middle of the corridor.|{b}'s boots squelch at every step, though it hasn't rained for a week.|{b}'s laces have tied themselves together, in a bow, very neatly.} You saw {a} flick a wand at them. So did nobody else. "Who did this?" {b} demands, of the corridor, and then of you.
text [c2d479] = {~{b}的靴子在走廊正中间自己跳起舞来。|{b}的靴子每走一步都咕叽作响，可已经一个星期没下雨了。|{b}的鞋带自己打成了一个蝴蝶结，打得还很整齐。}你看见{a}朝那双靴子弹了一下魔杖。别人都没看见。“谁干的？”{b}先冲着整条走廊质问，然后冲着你。
# en: A corridor where one student's boots are jigging along the flagstones on their own, the student in them furious, another student by a pillar looking innocent.
scene [28aacc] = 走廊里，一个学生的靴子自顾自地在石板上跳着吉格舞，穿靴子的人气急败坏，柱子旁另一个学生一脸无辜。
# en: Tell {b} the truth
opt.left.label [a0b54c] = 告诉{b}实话
# en: {~{b} stalks off to find {a}, still jigging. You hear about the rematch at supper.|{b} squelches off towards {a}. You don't stay to watch.|{b} hops away in search of {a}, still tied. There's a prefect involved by lunchtime.}
opt.left.say.1 [33d368] = {~{b}一边跳着一边气冲冲地去找{a}了。晚饭时你听说了这场报复战。|{b}咕叽咕叽地朝{a}走去。你没留下来看。|{b}单脚蹦着去找{a}，鞋带还绑着。到午饭时，已经惊动了级长。}
# en: "I didn't see a thing."
opt.right.label [52d462] = “我什么都没看见。”
# en: {~{b} dances off to the infirmary. {a} winks at you across the corridor, and you find you don't like being winked at.|{b} squelches away, sure it was someone else. {a} grins. It sits badly at lunch.|{b} sits on the floor and unpicks the bow with dignity. {a} owes you, and you'd rather {they} didn't.}
opt.right.say.1 [d6deb4] = {~{b}一路跳着去了医务室。{a}隔着走廊朝你眨了眨眼，你发现自己并不喜欢被人这样眨眼。|{b}咕叽咕叽地走了，认定是别人干的。{a}咧嘴一笑。这事让你午饭都吃得不是滋味。|{b}坐在地上，体面地解开了那个蝴蝶结。{a}欠了你一个人情，而你宁愿{they}没欠。}

@event between_not_them
# en: Not Them Again
title [4f6da4] = 又是他们
# en: {a} catches you at the common-room door. "Every time you sit with me, {b} comes too," {they} say{s}. "I'm not asking you to choose. I'm just asking you to stop bringing {b}." Over by the fire, {b} looks up, and smiles at you.
text [961c40] = {a}在公共休息室门口拦住了你。“每次你跟我坐，{b}都跟着来，”{they}说，“我不是要你选。我只是想请你别再带上{b}。”壁炉边，{b}抬起头，朝你笑了笑。
# en: Two students talking low in a common-room doorway, one glancing over the other's shoulder at a third by the fire.
scene [4edcd4] = 公共休息室门口，两个学生低声交谈，其中一人越过对方的肩膀，看向壁炉边的第三个人。
# en: Keep bringing {b}
opt.left.label [c143c8] = 照样带上{b}
# en: "Then I'll sit somewhere else," says {a}, and does, for a week. {b} never knows it was about {b.them}. You do.
opt.left.say.1 [32e55e] = “那我就坐别处去，”{a}说，接下来一个星期果真如此。{b}始终不知道这事跟{b.them}有关。你知道。
# en: Quietly leave {b} out
opt.right.label [c60d87] = 悄悄不带{b}
# en: You start meeting {a} in the library, where {b} never goes. It works. {b} asks once, cheerfully, where you've been. You say "around", which is true and isn't.
opt.right.say.1 [b8b569] = 你开始和{a}约在图书馆见面，{b}从来不去那儿。这招管用。{b}只问过一次，语气轻快，问你最近去哪儿了。你说“到处转转”——这话不假，却也不真。

@event between_peace_offering
# en: A Peace Offering
title [3e57a4] = 求和的礼物
# en: {a} has a parcel in one hand and a letter in the other, and the look of someone who's been carrying both for days. "It's for {b}. I was out of order. I said something I can't take back." {They} hold{s} them out. "Would you give {b.them} these? I can't face it."
text [d7d93a] = {a}一手拿着个包裹，一手拿着封信，看样子已经把这两样东西揣了好几天。“是给{b}的。我那次太过分了。我说了收不回来的话。”{they}把东西递过来。“你能替我交给{b.them}吗？我没脸去。”
# en: A student holding out a small wrapped parcel and a folded letter, looking anywhere but at the person they're handing it to.
scene [3d3a03] = 一个学生递出一个包好的小包裹和一封折好的信，眼睛看着哪儿都行，就是不看接东西的人。
# en: Make {a} do it in person
opt.left.label [fc12ae] = 让{a}亲自去
# en: You walk {a} there yourself, all the way to the door. It takes {them} two goes to say it. {b} takes the parcel, and then, after a long moment, takes {a}'s hand as well. You leave them to it.
opt.left.say.1 [768295] = 你亲自把{a}送了过去，一直送到门口。{they}试了两次才把话说出口。{b}接过包裹，过了好一会儿，又握住了{a}的手。你让他们俩自己说去了。
# en: {a} gets as far as the door, then turns round and walks straight back past you. "I said I couldn't," {they} say{s}. The parcel goes back in a drawer.
opt.left.say.2 [6857ca] = {a}走到门口，转身又从你身边径直走了回来。“我说了我做不到，”{they}说。包裹被放回了抽屉里。
# en: Carry it for {a}
opt.right.label [247acd] = 替{a}送去
# en: You spend the evening you meant for revising finding {b}, who reads the letter twice and says "Tell {a} I'll think about it." That's not nothing. You say so.
opt.right.say.1 [7b813c] = 你把原本用来复习的晚上花在了找{b}上。对方把信读了两遍，说：“告诉{a}，我会考虑的。”这不算什么都没有。你也这么转告了。

@event between_seconds
# en: Seconds
title [12f48a] = 决斗助手
# en: What started between {a} and {b} over a library book has become a matter of honour: a formal challenge, dawn, the far lawn. Neither will back down in front of the other, and {a} has asked you, of all people, to stand at {their} shoulder. "You only have to be there. And if I lose, don't let me hear about it."
text [6dfecd] = {a}和{b}之间起于一本图书馆借书的争执，如今成了荣誉之争：正式挑战，黎明，远处的草坪。两人谁也不肯在对方面前退让，而{a}偏偏请你站在{their}身边。“你只要在场就行。要是我输了，别让我听见有人提。”
# en: Dawn on a dew-wet lawn, two students with wands squared off, a third standing at one of their shoulders in the mist.
scene [168981] = 黎明时沾满露水的草坪上，两个学生举着魔杖对峙，第三个人站在其中一人肩旁，没在雾里。
# en: Stand as {a}'s second
opt.left.label [eaafe8] = 做{a}的决斗助手
# en: You stand at {a}'s shoulder {season == "winter": with your teeth chattering|in the wet grass}. The duel is short, loud and inconclusive; a stray hex singes your sleeve. {a} shakes your hand afterwards like a general. Half the dormitory heard about it by breakfast.
opt.left.say.1 [828aae] = 你站在{a}肩旁，{season == "winter": 冻得牙齿打战|脚下是湿漉漉的草}。决斗短促、吵闹、不分胜负；一道流弹咒烧焦了你的袖子。事后{a}像将军一样跟你握手。到早饭时，半个宿舍都听说了。
# en: Talk them out of it at dawn
opt.right.label [ce517c] = 黎明时劝他们罢手
# en: You stand between them on the {season == "winter": frosted|wet} grass and talk until the sun is up and both of them are too {season == "winter": cold|hungry} to want anything but toast. Nobody wins. Nobody has to.
opt.right.say.1 [4765b3] = 你站在{season == "winter": 结霜|湿漉漉}的草地上，挡在两人中间，一直说到太阳升起，说到他们{season == "winter": 冻|饿}得只想吃吐司。没人赢。也没人非得赢。
# en: They duel anyway, around you. You're not a very good wall. {a} says you embarrassed {them}, and {b} doesn't say anything, which is worse.
opt.right.say.2 [c68c0c] = 他们还是绕过你打了起来。你这堵墙不太称职。{a}说你让{them}丢了脸；{b}什么都没说，这更糟。

@event between_dorm_vote
# en: The Common-Room Vote
title [a858e1] = 公共休息室的投票
# en: {~The common room is voting on whether the window stays open at night. {a} is leading the cold side, {b} the warm one.|Today's vote: who gets the good armchair by the fire on exam weeks. {a} has a rota. {b} has a counter-rota.|The common room votes on the portrait over the fire, which snores: turned to the wall at night, or not. {a} says turn it. {b} would sooner die than agree with {a}.} It's tied. Your hand will decide it.
text [a956e2] = {~公共休息室正在投票表决夜里窗户开不开。{a}领着怕热的一方，{b}领着怕冷的一方。|今天的议题：考试周里壁炉边那把好扶手椅归谁。{a}排了张轮值表。{b}排了张反轮值表。|公共休息室要表决壁炉上方那幅会打呼噜的画像夜里要不要转过去面朝墙。{a}说转。{b}宁死也不肯同意{a}。}票数持平。你这一票说了算。
# en: A crowded common room, two students standing on chairs at opposite ends making speeches, everyone else holding up hands.
scene [ce48e2] = 挤满人的公共休息室，两个学生分别站在两头的椅子上发表演说，其他人都举着手。
# en: Vote with {a}
opt.left.label [5da9a4] = 投{a}一票
# en: {~The window stays open. {b} sleeps in two jumpers and a grievance.|The rota stands. {b} reads in the cold chair for a month and makes a point of it.|The portrait goes to the wall. {b} turns it back every night, on principle, and apologises to it.}
opt.left.say.1 [2ab723] = {~窗户开着。{b}穿着两件毛衣、揣着一肚子怨气睡觉。|轮值表照旧。{b}整整一个月都坐在冷椅子上看书，还特意让人看见。|画像转向了墙。{b}每晚都把它转回来，出于原则，还向它道歉。}
# en: Propose a middle way
opt.right.label [220580] = 提个折中的办法
# en: {~You say the window should be open on even nights, and suddenly that's what everyone wants. Nobody's happy. Everyone's slightly warmer.|You say the chair should go to whoever's actually revising. They both look at you as if you've cheated.|You say the portrait can face the wall on exam weeks only. It's a coward's answer. It passes unanimously, and the portrait sulks.}
opt.right.say.1 [b8fa0a] = {~你说窗户单数夜开、双数夜关——忽然大家都想要这个了。没人满意。每个人都暖和了一点。|你说椅子应该归真正在复习的人。两人看你的眼神，像你作了弊。|你说画像只在考试周面朝墙。这是个胆小鬼的答案。全票通过，画像生起了闷气。}

@event between_lab_partners
# en: A Bench for Three
title [5c410d] = 三人一张台
# en: {~The alchemy practical comes in threes, and the lecturer has put you at a bench with {a} and {b}.|A bench for three, a cauldron for one, and {a} and {b} have taken the two stools furthest from each other.|You're paired again: you, {a}, {b}, and one set of scales.} By the second step they're working two separate halves of the recipe and arguing through you.
text [576d6c] = {~炼金实践课三人一组，讲师把你和{a}、{b}分到了一张工作台。|三个人一张台，一口坩埚，{a}和{b}占了离彼此最远的两张凳子。|你又被分到一组：你、{a}、{b}，外加一杆秤。}到第二步时，他们已经各做各的半份配方，还隔着你吵架。
# en: A practical-class workbench, three students around one bubbling cauldron, two of them very carefully not passing each other anything.
scene [72c4ac] = 实践课的工作台，三个学生围着一口冒泡的坩埚，其中两个小心翼翼地不给对方递任何东西。
# en: Do the work yourself
opt.left.label [c9d72d] = 自己全包了
# en: {~You do the whole thing while they sulk at the ends of the bench. It comes out right, and you're the only one who knows how.|You make the brew. They each claim the other ruined it. It's perfectly fine.|You weigh, stir and bottle it alone. The mark is good. The afternoon was not.}
opt.left.say.1 [e8d77f] = {~他们在台子两头生闷气，你一个人把活全干了。药剂做对了，而只有你知道是怎么做成的。|你熬好了药。他们都说是对方搞砸的。其实完全没问题。|你一个人称量、搅拌、装瓶。分数不错。这个下午不怎么样。}
# en: Make them work it together
opt.right.label [aa3269] = 让他们一起干
# en: {~You give {a} the stirring and {b} the timing, and make them call it out to each other. It works. The cauldron doesn't explode, and neither do they.|You hand each of them one half of the recipe. They have to read it out to each other. By the end {b} is laughing at {a}'s pronunciation, and {a} lets {b.them}.|You stand back with your arms folded until they talk. Eventually, they do. The brew is late, and passable.}
opt.right.say.1 [7425fc] = {~你让{a}负责搅拌，{b}负责计时，逼他们互相报数。成了。坩埚没炸，他们俩也没炸。|你把配方一人一半分给他们。他们只能互相念给对方听。到最后，{b}笑话起{a}的发音，{a}也由着{b.them}笑。|你抱着胳膊站在一旁，直到他们开口说话。最终，他们开口了。药剂交晚了，勉强及格。}
# en: {~They won't. The brew curdles while they argue about whose fault the curdling is.|The cauldron boils over while {a} and {b} each wait for the other to move it.|You try to referee and the brew goes grey. All three of you get the same bad mark, which is, at least, fair.}
opt.right.say.2 [1c5f37] = {~他们就是不肯。药剂结了块，他们还在争是谁害它结了块。|{a}和{b}都等着对方去挪坩埚，结果它沸溢了。|你想当裁判，药剂却变成了灰色。三个人拿了同样的低分，至少还算公平。}

@event between_cipher
# en: The Cipher
title [a7fefb] = 密码
# en: {a} and {b} have been passing notes all term in a cipher of their own: little drawings that stand for words, a fish for "boring", a tower for "Thorne". You've watched them laugh at blank-looking scraps for weeks. Today {b} slides one to you. It's the key, and it's a test.
text [4f45be] = {a}和{b}整个学期都在用自创的密码传纸条：用小图画代表词语，一条鱼代表“无聊”，一座塔代表“索恩”。你已经看着他们对着看似空白的纸片笑了好几个星期。今天，{b}把一张纸条推给了你。是密码表，也是一场考验。
# en: Two students passing a scrap of paper covered in little drawn symbols under a lecture-hall desk, a third leaning in to see.
scene [d9463c] = 讲堂的课桌底下，两个学生传递着一张画满小符号的纸片，第三个人凑过去看。
# en: Learn the cipher
opt.left.label [1d4945] = 学会这套密码
# en: You spend a lecture you should have spent listening learning forty little drawings instead. By the end of it you can read "this lecture is a fish" from three rows away, and the three of you have a language nobody else in the castle speaks.
opt.left.say.1 [80da79] = 本该听课的一节课，你用来记了四十个小图画。下课时，你已经能隔着三排读出“这节课是条鱼”，而你们三个有了一门全城堡没人会说的语言。
# en: Hand the key back
opt.right.label [939e43] = 把密码表还回去
# en: "I'd only get you both caught," you whisper, and slide it back. {b} shrugs and tucks it away. The notes go on passing in front of you, and you find you'd quite like to know what the fish means.
opt.right.say.1 [fc8a85] = “我只会害你们俩被抓，”你小声说，把纸条推了回去。{b}耸耸肩，把它收好了。纸条照样在你面前传来传去，你发现自己挺想知道那条鱼是什么意思的。

@event between_inside_joke
# en: The Joke You Missed
title [c90ac9] = 你没听懂的笑话
# en: {~{b} says "the goose" and {a} has to sit down on the stairs.|{a} hums four notes and {b} snorts tea out of {b.their} nose.|{b} only has to raise one eyebrow and {a} is gone, helpless, crying with laughter.} You have no idea why. They look at you, still laughing, and wait to see what you'll do.
text [ad340c] = {~{b}说了句“那只鹅”，{a}笑得只好在楼梯上坐下。|{a}哼了四个音，{b}一口茶从鼻子里喷了出来。|{b}只消挑一下眉毛，{a}就彻底不行了，笑得眼泪直流。}你完全不知道为什么。他们看着你，还在笑，等着看你怎么办。
# en: Two students doubled over with laughter on a staircase, a third standing a step below them, smiling politely.
scene [ebf5ae] = 两个学生在楼梯上笑弯了腰，第三个人站在下面一级台阶上，礼貌地微笑着。
# en: Laugh along anyway
opt.left.label [b615b4] = 跟着笑就是了
# en: {~You laugh. {a} can tell you've no idea, and it's somehow worse than not laughing.|You laugh along, too late and a bit too loud. {b} pats your shoulder, kindly.|You laugh. They stop. "You don't know, do you," says {a}, delighted.}
opt.left.say.1 [7fac4c] = {~你笑了。{a}看得出你根本不懂，这不知怎么比不笑还糟。|你跟着笑，笑晚了，还笑得太大声。{b}好心地拍了拍你的肩。|你笑了。他们停了下来。“你不知道，对吧，”{a}说，乐坏了。}
# en: "All right. What's the joke?"
opt.right.label [3bcf3b] = “好吧。到底什么这么好笑？”
# en: {~It takes ten minutes to explain, involves a professor and a pond, and is about half as funny told. But now it's yours too, and the next time someone says "the goose" you're the one on the stairs.|They explain. It's about you, a little, from your first week. You laugh anyway. It is quite funny.|"You had to be there," says {b}. Then they make you be there, re-enacting the whole thing on the landing. You're in it now.}
opt.right.say.1 [e161a3] = {~解释花了十分钟，牵扯到一位教授和一个池塘，讲出来只有一半好笑。可现在它也是你的了，下次有人说“那只鹅”，坐在楼梯上的就是你。|他们解释了。这事有一点是关于你的，出自你入学第一周。你还是笑了。确实挺好笑。|“你得在场才懂，”{b}说。然后他们硬是让你“在场”了一回，在楼梯平台上把整件事重演了一遍。现在你也是其中一员了。}

@event between_cover_roll
# en: Answering for Two
title [f14798] = 替人答到
# en: Roll call. {b}'s seat is empty, and {a} leans over. "{b}'s not ill, {b}'s just not here. One more absence and it's a letter home. Answer for {b.them}? I can't, the lecturer knows my voice." The lecturer is three names away.
text [779176] = 点名了。{b}的座位空着，{a}凑过来。“{b}没病，{b}只是不在。再缺一次，信就要寄回家了。你替{b.them}答一声？我不行，讲师认得我的声音。”讲师离这儿还有三个名字。
# en: A lecture hall at the start of class, a lecturer reading names from a register, two students whispering in the third row beside an empty seat.
scene [51fc7b] = 上课伊始的讲堂，讲师照着名册点名，第三排一张空座位旁，两个学生在窃窃私语。
# en: "Present."
opt.left.label [98e6cc] = “到。”
# en: You say it in a voice you hope is {b}'s. The lecturer ticks the register without looking up. {b} buys you both breakfast for a week and never tells you where {b.they} went.
opt.left.say.1 [fff9d7] = 你用一种希望像{b}的声音答了。讲师头也没抬就在名册上打了勾。{b}请你们俩吃了一个星期的早饭，却始终没告诉你{b.they}去了哪儿。
# en: The lecturer looks up. "Remarkable, how {b} has your face." It's a detention for you and the letter home for {b} anyway. {b} is grateful, which almost makes it worse.
opt.left.say.2 [240daf] = 讲师抬起头。“真神奇，{b}居然长着你的脸。”你被罚了禁闭，{b}的信照样寄回了家。{b}很感激你，这几乎让事情更糟。
# en: Stay quiet
opt.right.label [9adbd8] = 不出声
# en: The name hangs in the air and goes down as absent. {a} doesn't look at you for the rest of the lecture. {b} gets the letter home, and turns up the next day, and never asks who didn't answer.
opt.right.say.1 [c12f86] = 那个名字悬在半空，被记作缺席。整节课{a}都没再看你一眼。家信寄了出去；第二天{b}照常来上课，从没问过是谁没替{b.them}答到。

@event between_closed_table
# en: Saved, Sorry
title [f46bb5] = 有人了，抱歉
# en: You, {a} and {b} have had the corner table for a term now. A first-year with a full tray and nowhere to sit asks if the fourth place is free. {a} and {b} exchange a look you know very well. It means "no".
text [851766] = 你、{a}和{b}占着角落那张桌子已经一个学期了。一个端着满满一盘饭、无处可坐的一年级生问第四个位子有没有人。{a}和{b}交换了一个你再熟悉不过的眼神。意思是“没空”。
# en: A corner table in a busy refectory, three friends crowded round it, a first-year with a full tray hovering at the end.
scene [5a8f9f] = 热闹膳堂里的一张角落餐桌，三个朋友挤在桌边，一个端着满盘饭菜的一年级生在桌尾踌躇。
# en: Make room
opt.left.label [d02342] = 腾个位子
# en: You shove your bag along and the first-year sits down, pink with relief. {a} is cool for the length of the meal. By the end of the week the first-year is the one who saves the table, and {b} has adopted them.
opt.left.say.1 [d2c528] = 你把书包往边上一推，那个一年级生坐了下来，如释重负，脸都红了。整顿饭{a}都有点冷淡。到周末时，已经是那个一年级生在给大家占桌子了，而{b}也把这孩子收作了小跟班。
# en: "Sorry, it's saved."
opt.right.label [c762ea] = “抱歉，有人了。”
# en: The first-year says "oh, of course," and walks away very straight. The corner stays yours. {a} relaxes. You find you can't remember what the fourth place was ever saved for.
opt.right.say.1 [d25e43] = 一年级生说了句“哦，当然”，挺直了背走开了。角落还是你们的。{a}放松下来。你发现自己想不起来，第四个位子当初到底是给谁留的。

@event between_night_roof
# en: The Roof at Night
title [b7c65d] = 夜里的屋顶
# en: {~After lights out, {a} and {b} are halfway out of the window with a blanket and a stolen jar of honey.|{a} taps on your door after lights out. {b} is behind {them}, holding the roof-ladder.|It's a clear night, and {a} and {b} are already on the roof. A hand appears at your window.} "Come on," whispers {a}. "The stars are out and the prefects aren't."
text [61cb97] = {~熄灯之后，{a}和{b}抱着一条毯子和一罐偷来的蜂蜜，已经翻出了半个窗户。|熄灯后，{a}敲响你的门。{b}跟在{them}身后，扛着上屋顶的梯子。|今夜晴朗，{a}和{b}早已上了屋顶。一只手出现在你的窗前。}“快来，”{a}低声说，“星星出来了，级长没出来。”
# en: Two students halfway out of a dormitory window onto a moonlit roof, one of them holding a hand back in for a third.
scene [55e92a] = 月光下，两个学生已经半个身子探出宿舍窗外、爬上屋顶，其中一人把手伸回窗内，去拉第三个人。
# en: Climb out with them
opt.left.label [185840] = 跟他们爬出去
# en: {~You sit on the slates until the honey's gone and you've named three constellations wrongly. Tomorrow will be long. You don't mind yet.|Up on the roof the three of you say the kind of things you only say at that height. You're tired all the next day, and it was worth it.|The slates are freezing and the view is everything. You get in at three, and nobody catches you, this time.}
opt.left.say.1 [6e3b65] = {~你们坐在石板瓦上，直到蜂蜜吃光，认错了三个星座。明天会很漫长。眼下你还不在乎。|在屋顶上，你们三个说了些只有在那个高度才说得出口的话。第二天你困了一整天，值了。|石板瓦冰凉，景色却是一切。你凌晨三点才溜回来，这一回没人抓到你。}
# en: Stay in bed
opt.right.label [50ea64] = 待在床上
# en: {~You hear them laughing above your ceiling until late. You sleep well. You're not sure that was the point.|"Your loss," whispers {b}, and the window clicks shut. In the morning they share a look you aren't in.|You stay warm. At breakfast they're yawning and pleased with themselves, and talk about something called "the honey incident".}
opt.right.say.1 [f6da30] = {~你听见他们在你天花板上面笑到很晚。你睡得很好。你不确定这是不是重点。|“你的损失，”{b}小声说，窗户咔哒一声关上了。早上，他们交换了一个你不在其中的眼神。|你暖暖和和的。早饭时他们哈欠连天，又得意洋洋，聊着一件叫“蜂蜜事件”的事。}

@event between_falling_out
# en: Both Sides
title [4e9822] = 各执一词
# en: {a} and {b} have fallen out, properly, over something neither will name. Each has told you their side by lunchtime, and the sides don't match. "You'll tell {b} I'm right," says {a}. An hour later, {b} says exactly the same about {a}.
text [0cf4c6] = {a}和{b}闹翻了，闹得很僵，为的是一件谁都不肯说的事。到午饭时，两人都跟你讲了各自的说法，而说法对不上。“你会告诉{b}我是对的，”{a}说。一小时后，{b}说起{a}，一字不差。
# en: Two students on opposite sides of a cloister garden, arms folded, each with their back to the other, a third student on the path between them.
scene [728282] = 回廊花园的两侧站着两个学生，都抱着胳膊，背对着彼此，第三个学生站在他们之间的小径上。
# en: Get them in one room
opt.left.label [0e8eb1] = 把他们弄到一个屋里
# en: You book the window seat in the library and send each of them a note that says the other one is sorry. Neither was. By the time they've worked that out, they're arguing about you instead, and it's over.
opt.left.say.1 [47759e] = 你订下了图书馆的窗边座，给两人各送了张纸条，说对方很抱歉。其实谁都没有。等他们弄明白这一点，已经转而一起数落你了——事情也就过去了。
# en: They come, see each other, and leave by different doors. Each of them thinks you took the other's side. For a while you have two friends who don't talk to each other, and both talk to you about it.
opt.left.say.2 [405dee] = 他们来了，看见彼此，从两扇门各自走了。两人都以为你站在了对方那边。有一阵子，你有了两个彼此不说话、却都跟你说这件事的朋友。
# en: Stay out of it
opt.right.label [03d24a] = 置身事外
# en: You listen to both, agree with neither, and keep your head down. It takes them a month to start speaking again, and they never quite sit together the way they did. You wonder sometimes if a word from you would have done it.
opt.right.say.1 [7dce05] = 你两边都听，谁也不附和，低着头过日子。他们花了一个月才重新说上话，再也没像从前那样坐在一起。有时你会想，要是你说一句话，会不会就好了。
